=== Jacob loved, Esau hated
--- Malachi 1 & Romans 9: Jacob Loved, Esau Hated [title]
overline: Malachi 1 & Romans 9
sub: Jacob Loved, Esau Hated • Nations, Not Individuals

--- Malachi 1:1-3
scripture: Malachi 1:1-3
scripture: Romans 9:13

--- Genesis 25:21-23: The Prophecy
scripture: Genesis 25:21-23

--- The Meaning of "Loved" and "Hated"
p: In Scripture, "loved" and "hated" often mean preferred over or chosen above the other, not absolute personal hatred or despising as we use the words today.
p: Jesus said:
scripture: Luke 14:26
p: This does not mean literal hatred or despising of family, but to love and prefer Christ more than all others — to place Him first.
p: In the same way, "I loved Jacob, but Esau have I hated" means God preferred/chose Jacob's line over Esau's — not that He personally despised Esau as an individual.

--- The Context: Nations, Not Individuals
- The reference is not to the individual twins at birth in Genesis 25.
- It is not about God hating and condemning Esau personally in the womb.
- Esau was reconciled with Jacob, received his own blessing from Isaac, and lived prosperously (Genesis 33; 36).
- The statement concerns the nations that came from them: Israel (Jacob) vs. Edom (Esau).
- Edom (the nation) fell away, refused to help Israel in the wilderness, rejoiced in Judah’s captivity, and was ultimately destroyed ~1400 years later (Obadiah; [[Malachi 1:4]]).
- Paul uses this to show: God’s choice of one nation/line over another (by grace, not works) is consistent — just as Gentiles are now grafted in while partial hardness came to unbelieving Jews (Romans 11).

--- God Judges Individuals by Faith • Nations by Works
- God judges individuals according to faith — whether they believe and call upon the Lord ([[Romans 10:13]]; [[Genesis 4:26]]).
- Nations are judged here on earth according to their works and collective actions — obedience or rebellion, mercy or enmity (e.g., Edom’s treatment of Israel led to national judgment).
p: Esau personally was blessed and reconciled; Edom as a nation was judged for its unbelief and hostility.
p: The same principle applies in Romans: believing Jews and Gentiles are saved by faith; the unbelieving nation faced hardening and partial blindness.

--- Jacob and Esau in summary
point: Preferred, not despised | The phrase "Jacob have I loved, but Esau have I hated" refers to God’s preference of one nation over another — not personal hatred of Esau as an individual.
point: "Loved" and "hated" | Here they mean preferred/chosen above, just as Jesus taught to "hate" family in comparison to loving Him supremely.
point: Faith and works | Individuals are judged by faith; nations are judged here on earth by their works.
take: Paul’s point: God’s choice of lines/nations by grace is biblical — consistent with the gospel to the Gentiles and the pattern throughout Scripture.

=== God's pattern in Scripture
--- God's Pattern in Scripture [title]
overline: God's Pattern of Choosing the Weak & Foolish
sub: Choosing the Weak, the Foolish, the Younger, the Unexpected

--- God hath chosen
scripture: 1 Corinthians 1:27-29
p: Throughout Scripture we see the same repeated pattern: God deliberately chooses what man considers weak, foolish, or unlikely, so that His power and purpose are unmistakably displayed and all boasting is excluded.

--- Chosen: the firstborn and the older (1)
table: What Man Expects / Esteems | What God Chooses | Key Reference
tr: Cain (firstborn) | Abel, then Seth | Genesis 4:4-5; 4:25
tr: Ishmael (firstborn son of Abraham) | Isaac | Genesis 17:18-21; 21:12; Romans 9:7-9
tr: Esau (firstborn, stronger) | Jacob | Genesis 25:23; Malachi 1:2-3; Romans 9:10-13
tr: Zerah (hand came out first) | Perez | Genesis 38:27-30
tr: Reuben, Simeon, Levi (older brothers) | Judah (kingship) & Joseph (birthright) | Genesis 49:3-10; 1 Chronicles 5:1-2
tr: Manasseh (firstborn of Joseph) | Ephraim | Genesis 48:13-20

--- Chosen: leaders and kings (2)
table: What Man Expects / Esteems | What God Chooses | Key Reference
tr: Aaron (older brother) | Moses (as primary leader) | Exodus 4:14-16; 7:1-2
tr: Firstborn sons of Israel | Levites | Numbers 3:12-13; 8:16-18
tr: Eliab & older brothers of David | David (youngest shepherd) | 1 Samuel 16:6-13
tr: Saul (tall, kingly stature) | David | 1 Samuel 9-16
tr: Adonijah (older, attempted throne) | Solomon | 1 Kings 1:5-53; 2:15
tr: Vashti (queen) | Esther (Jewish orphan) | Esther 1-2

--- Chosen: the parables and Christ (3)
table: What Man Expects / Esteems | What God Chooses | Key Reference
tr: Early laborers (worked longer) | Late hires receive same reward | Matthew 20:1-16
tr: Rejected stone | Becomes cornerstone | Psalm 118:22; Matthew 21:42
tr: Original wedding guests | Outsiders from highways | Matthew 22:1-14
tr: Chief priests & elders | Publicans & harlots enter first | Matthew 21:31
tr: Children of the kingdom (by birth) | New believing nation | Matthew 21:43; 8:11-12

--- Chosen: humble and last (4)
table: What Man Expects / Esteems | What God Chooses | Key Reference
tr: Pharisee | Publican (tax collector) justified | Luke 18:9-14
tr: Rich man | Lazarus (beggar) comforted | Luke 16:19-31
tr: Elder brother | Prodigal younger son welcomed | Luke 15:11-32
tr: Israel after the flesh | Children of the promise | Romans 9:6-8; Galatians 4:28-31

--- More examples, in rough biblical order (1)
p: This theme of God choosing the weak, foolish, and unexpected runs throughout the entire Bible.
p: Here are many more examples in rough biblical order:
- Japheth > Shem (blessing order – Gen 9)
- Leah > Rachel in fruitfulness (Gen 29–30)
- First wilderness generation > Second generation (entered land – Num/Deut)
- Pharaoh's firstborn > Israel's firstborn spared (Ex 12)
- Egypt > Exodus nation freed (Ex)
- Northern Kingdom (larger) > Southern Kingdom (Judah preserved – history/Kings)
- Othniel > Judahite elders (Judg 1)
- Ehud > Previous leaders (Judg 3)
- Sisera/Barak > Deborah & Jael (Judg 4–5)
- Gideon > Midianite oppressors (Judg 6–8)
- Jephthah > His elder brothers & Ammon (Judg 11)
- Samson > Parents' expectations & Philistines (Judg 13–16)

--- More examples (2)
- Nearer kinsman > Boaz as redeemer (Ruth)
- Michal > Abigail as favored wife (1–2 Sam)
- Nabal > David (1 Sam 25)
- Saul's house > David's (1–2 Sam)
- Eli's priestly line > Samuel & Zadok (1 Sam)
- Benjamites > Judah royal line (history)
- Jeroboam’s line > Baasha > Zimri > Omri > Jehu (1–2 Kings)
- Ahaziah/Athaliah > Joash preserved (2 Kings)
- Amon > Josiah (2 Kings)
- Various wicked kings > Godly successors (Kings/Chronicles)
- Elijah > Elisha (double portion – 2 Kings 2)
- Abiathar > Zadok (priestly line)
- Hophni/Phinehas > Faithful line
- Unworthy priests > Samuel

--- More examples (3)
- Jonah's desire > Nineveh repents (Jonah)
- False prophets > True prophets (prophets)
- "Divorced" Northern Israel > Judah warned (prophets)
- Corporate Israel > Remnant (Isaiah etc.)
- Babylon/tyrants > Persia/Cyrus as anointed (Isa 45)
- Dry bones > Revived army (Ezek 37)
- Bad figs > Good figs preserved (Jer 24)
- Unfruitful vineyard > New keepers (Isa 5; parables)
- Old covenant shadows > New covenant reality (Hebrews)
- High places > Lowly exalted (teaching)
- Exalted humbled > Humble exalted
- Greatest > Servant/child
- Dominant tribes > Various judges
- Jebusites > David conquering Jerusalem
- And many more throughout the Word...

--- The glory goes to Him alone
lead: The Bible consistently shows God delighting to use what the world counts as nothing so that all the glory goes to Him alone and no flesh can boast.
take: The first shall be last and the last shall be first.

